{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:purely-periodic-support","Purely periodic support","<p><em> For\nintegers <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, and every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi></math></span>-periodic <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> (i.e. <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⇔</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></math></span> for all\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span>) containing a positive element,\nthe support series <span class=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> is irrational. <strong>Hypotheses:</strong>\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi></math></span> <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi></math></span>-periodic, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:eventually-periodic-support","Eventually periodic support","<p><em> For\nintegers <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, if <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> is infinite and its\nmembership is <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi></math></span>-periodic from some\nthreshold <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span> on (i.e. <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⇔</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></math></span> for all\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>), then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> is\nirrational, by transferring irrationality across the finite symmetric\ndifference from the shifted purely periodic set <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>pure</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>. <strong>Hypotheses:</strong> <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, eventual <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi></math></span>-periodicity from <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi></math></span> infinite.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:residue-class-support","Residue-class support","<p><em> For\nintegers <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi></math></span>, the series <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>n</mi><mo>≡</mo><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.444em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> is irrational.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:odd-support","Odd support","<p><em> For every integer\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, the series <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>n</mi><mtext> odd</mtext></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>\nis irrational. This density-<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>\nsupport is the case treated explicitly by Luca and Tachiya <span\nclass=\"citation\" data-cites=\"lucatachiya2017\"><a href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#ref-3\">[3]</a></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:topology","Achievement-set topology and measure","<p><em> <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span> (Definition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#defn:half-question\">5.2</a>) is compact,\nclosed, perfect, totally disconnected, and nowhere dense; its Lebesgue\nmeasure is exactly <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>: <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>volume</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>.\n<strong>Hypotheses:</strong> none. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span> is a Cantor set of positive\nmeasure, often called a fat Cantor set. Its measure is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>, not the length of its ambient interval\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span>, where <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>E</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.6067</mn></math></span>. Strict separation and\nsummability give the compactness, unique coding, and Cantor topology,\nand Hornich’s strict-tail theorem, as proved by Nitecki <span\nclass=\"citation\" data-cites=\"nitecki2013\"><a href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#ref-4\">[4]</a></span>, gives the measure as\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo movablelimits=\"true\">lim</mo><mi>N</mi></munder><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span>: each level-<span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi></math></span> cylinder has length <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></math></span>, and the <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup></math></span> disjoint cylinders have total length\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></math></span>. The Mersenne-specific input\nis <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Other weight\nsequences require their own tail asymptotic.<br />\n<em>Use:</em> Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:greedy-survival-record\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:greedy-survival-record\">5.10</a> below\n(compactness is exactly what powers every “limit of achieved points is\nachieved” argument used downstream, including the seam-limit route of\nPart 2).</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:greedy-survival-record","Membership equals greedy survival at\nevery level","<p><em> For a real <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></span>,\nlet <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> be its greedy remainder\nafter rank <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span>, and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></math></span>, as in the initial\nnotation. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext> </mtext><mtext>and</mtext><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mtext> for every </mtext><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The\ninequality says that the unselected tail has enough total mass at every\nrank. A failure at any one rank excludes membership; the inequalities at\nall ranks give a representation.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:master-identity","Decomposition of the integer remainder","<p><em> For every quotient row <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>6</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable minlabelspacing=\"0em\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mtr><mtd><mtext>(</mtext><mtext>I</mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo>=</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo>+</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><mi>Skip</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Here the take and skip sets, capacity and\nremainder are those defined at the start of this subsection. The\nidentity separates the contribution of the full exponent range from that\nof the omitted ranks. It makes no claim that there are few omitted\nranks; controlling their sum is a separate part of any\napplication.<br />\n<strong>Proof.</strong> For <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></math></span> the\nquotient is <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, and for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></math></span> it is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></math></span>. Hence </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Substitute this in the\ndefinition of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, split <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⊔</mo><msub><mrow><mi>Skip</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span>,\nand use <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. This gives (I). <strong>Certification:</strong>\nrows <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>6</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>200</mn></math></span> rechecked with\nzero mismatches by an exact <a\nhref=\"https://github.com/wcook04/plectis-erdos/blob/f757115e03bebe5c04431a147da559015c680a37/research/experiments/erdos257/check_master_identity.py\">program</a>\n(§<a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#ssec:anchors\">5.7</a>).<br />\n<em>Use:</em> (I) permits substitution of information about <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. A resulting estimate for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span> must also retain or bound the\nskip-set sum; information about <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi></math></span>\nalone need not control their cancellation.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:real-form","Real (non-integer) form of the quotient\nidentity","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and define the constant </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>C</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo>:=</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo>−</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo>=</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo>=</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mn>0.1066951524152917</mn><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> where <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>E</mi></math></span> is the Erdős–Borwein constant. Then,\nfor every quotient row <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>6</mn></math></span>,\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable minlabelspacing=\"0em\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mtr><mtd><mtext>(</mtext><mtext>II</mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo>=</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">(</mo></mrow><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><mi>Skip</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo>−</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo>+</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2.</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p><em> <strong>Proof.</strong> Splitting the full\nseries at <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span> gives </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The\ntail bounds <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><msup><mn>4</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> show <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Use of the error bound.</strong> For <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, the condition <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><mi>Skip</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></math></span>\nis sufficient for <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span>.\nConversely, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span> implies\n<span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><mi>Skip</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></math></span>.\nThus a square-root-exponential deviation corresponds to a separation of\norder <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>, with the displayed\nadditive error retained. This is not an exact equivalence after simply\ndiscarding that error.<br />\n<strong>Note:</strong> <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>\nis Mersenne-specific, but the shape “scaled deviation <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo>=</mo></math></span> <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>⋅</mo></math></span>(finite skip-sum <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo>−</mo></math></span>\ntarget constant) <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo>+</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>” is a\ntemplate, not yet matched to any analogous constant on the #249\nside.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:dynamics","Exact recurrences for the three\nbranches","<p><em> Fix <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span>. Among\nthe quotient sums over subsets of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>, let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span> give the largest sum at most <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span> and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span> give the smallest sum strictly\ngreater than <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span>. Thus <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span> is the greedy support already\ndefined. Put </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>o</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> and define the two correction terms by </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Both\nlie in <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span>, since <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> cannot divide both consecutive\nintegers. The next remainder is </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing=\"1em\" rowspacing=\".2em\" columnalign=\"left left\"><mtr><mtd><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>o</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mtext>if </mtext><mn>4</mn><mi>o</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>4</mn><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mtext>if not </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi></mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mn>4</mn><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>4</mn><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" symmetric=\"true\"></mo></mrow></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Consequently, for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></math></span>,\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable minlabelspacing=\"0em\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mtr><mtd><mtext>(</mtext><mtext>III</mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing=\"1em\" rowspacing=\".2em\" columnalign=\"left left\"><mtr><mtd><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>o</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" symmetric=\"true\"></mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:fatal-absorbing","Infinitely many greedy skips force\nmembership","<p><em> Fix <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. If\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span> at some rank <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span>, every later rank is selected and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></math></span> for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. Consequently, infinitely many\nomitted positive ranks imply <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:seam-limit","The quotient remainders converge to the\ngreedy deficit","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>G</mi></math></span> be the\nreal greedy support for <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, and\nlet <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math></span> be the integer-greedy\nsupport at row <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi></math></span>. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟶</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>s</mi></msup></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟶</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> In particular, the following\nconditions are equivalent: <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>; the full sequence <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>s</mi></msup></math></span> tends to zero; and\nthere exist <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:two-channel-cap","Two-channel and dyadic cap sufficiency","<p><em> Suppose that for every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>\nat which the next weight is skipped, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mn>4</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>. The stronger bound\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></math></span> at the same\nskipped ranks also suffices. The first bound retains two positive\ngeometric terms of the full tail; it is larger, and hence less\nrestrictive, than the second. Both are sufficient tests on the specified\ngreedy orbit. The result does not assert that either bound holds at all\nits skipped ranks.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:second-channel","Second-channel phase separation\nsufficiency","<p><em> Define <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math></span>. If\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>37</mn><mn>56</mn></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>|</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mtext>for every </mtext><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> then\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>. These are exact\nrational inequalities because the target and every finite greedy\nremainder are rational. The cited finite calculation establishes them\nfor <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></math></span>; it therefore\nsuffices to prove them for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Unlike Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:two-channel-cap\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:two-channel-cap\">6.3</a>, this\nhypothesis concerns every positive rank, not only skipped ranks. It\nexcludes an explicit interval around <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span>. For example, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> implies the displayed\ninequality when <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, but no such\navoidance theorem for the whole orbit is proved here.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:straddle-closed-set","Straddle-prefix closed-set criterion","<p><em> Suppose that for every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>\nthere is a finite support <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> such that\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThen <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>. The supports\nneed not agree at different depths: compactness, together with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, supplies a limiting support.\nConversely, truncating a support representing <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span> supplies such finite supports. Thus\nthis condition is an exact finite-approximation formulation of\nmembership, not a criterion already known to hold for an unresolved\ntarget.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:largest-skip-late","Keeping the largest skipped rank beyond\ntwo thirds","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> be the\nsupport of the integer-greedy row defined in Section <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#ssec:seam-model\">5.4</a>. Assume the\nfollowing implication for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>14</mn></math></span> and every largest omitted rank\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>: </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>d</mi><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>d</mi><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mtext>or</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>. The hypothesis says\nthat either the same omitted rank remains beyond two thirds of the next\nrow, or the next row omits its terminal rank. The latter alternative is\nexactly an upper or middle transition in the preceding\nclassification.</em></p>\n<p><em>The cited finite calculation establishes the initial inequality\nat row <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>14</mn></math></span>. The displayed\nimplication then preserves the inequality by induction and supplies\nomitted ranks tending to infinity. It is an unproved condition on every\nlater row; the verified base case alone does not establish it.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:middle-producer-escape","Two sufficient bounds at middle\ntransitions","<p><em> Suppose that at every middle transition with row\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>13</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThen <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>. The stronger\ncondition <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mi>s</mi></math></span> at\nall such rows also suffices.</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.json?v=09745a86500d"}
